    "math_academy_id": "q-129389",
    "knowledge_point_id": "8a3752a0-431d-5520-97b1-493c36bbce54",
    "knowledge_point": "Computing the Variance of a Scaled Random Variable",
    "problem": "The probability distribution of the random variable $X$ is given below. Given that $\\text{E}[X]=-\\frac{1}{2},$ calculate $\\text{Var}[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}X].$ \n\n| $x$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |",
    "worked_solution": "Recall that for any random variable $X$ and constant $a,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[aX]={a}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X].\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}X] & ={(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can compute the variance of $X$ using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2}\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we're given that $\\text{E}[X]=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ \n\nWe start by computing $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}].$ Summing up the products of each value of ${X}^{2}$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & =(-2)^{2}\\cdot f(-2)+(-1)^{2}\\cdot f(-1)+{1}^{2}\\cdot f(1)+{2}^{2}\\cdot f(2) \\\\  & =4(\\frac{1}{2})+1(\\frac{1}{8})+1(\\frac{1}{8})+4(\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{4}-{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{4}-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}X] & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
